## 8. Step 4 — TEE custody (deferred; verified facts for later)
OutLayer Agent Custody (hackathon partner): agent holds only an API key (`wk_…`); key derivation, signing and policy evaluation run inside Intel TDX; master secret from the NEAR MPC network; policy (per-tx/hourly/daily limits, whitelist, rate limit, multisig, freeze) stored encrypted on chain. `POST /register` returns `api_key` + implicit `near_account_id`. Testnet base URL `https://testnet-api.outlayer.ai`; transfer, `call`, balance, policy work on testnet; Intents routes are mainnet-only. Cumulative caps are best-effort under concurrency → serialize agent payments. Source: app.outlayer.ai/docs/agent-custody. In step 4, `services/custody` implements the `chain-core` signer interface against `/wallet/v1/call` and replaces the dev signer in the private-ai scripts.

## 9. Acceptance criteria
1. With the paywall on `PAY_RAILS=near` (testnet), one real paid call appears in the console as a `claim` and as a chain transfer, and reconciles to `matched` within the grace window.
2. Killing the neardata road still records the transfer via `qn-rpc`; `/api/feeds` shows which road won and lags.
3. Restarting near-watch backfills from the stored cursor with no duplicates.
4. A claim with no chain transfer becomes `only_in_log` after grace; an x402 USDC transfer to pay-to (no memo needed) with no claim becomes `only_on_ledger`.
5. No secret or token-bearing URL appears in logs, API responses or the browser.
6. README "do X → see Y" steps reproduce 1–4 on a fresh machine.